<article class="env-lemma" id="0BBI">
  <p><a class="environment-identifier" href="/tag/0BBI">Lemma <span data-tag="0BBI">10.70.8</span>.</a> Let $R$ be a ring, $I \subset R$ an ideal, $a \in I$, and $f \in R$. Set $R' = R[\frac{I}{a}]$ and $R'' = R[\frac{fI}{fa}]$. Then there is a surjective $R$-algebra map $R' \to R''$ whose kernel is the set of $f$-power torsion elements of $R'$. </p>
</article><article class="env-proof">
    <p>
      <strong>Proof.</strong>
      The map is given by sending $x/a^ n$ for $x \in I^ n$ to $f^ nx/(fa)^ n$. It is straightforward to check this map is well defined and surjective. Since $af$ is a nonzero divisor in $R''$ (Lemma <a href="/tag/07Z3" data-tag="07Z3">10.70.2</a>) we see that the set of $f$-power torsion elements are mapped to zero. Conversely, if $x \in R'$ and $f^ n x \not= 0$ for all $n > 0$, then $(af)^ n x \not= 0$ for all $n$ as $a$ is a nonzero divisor in $R'$. It follows that the image of $x$ in $R''$ is not zero by the description of $R''$ following Definition <a href="/tag/052Q" data-tag="052Q">10.70.1</a>. 
      <span class="qed">$\square$</span>
    </p>
</article>